I think that’s a fruitful direction if you broaden “mirror” from a physical object to a more general transformation that preserves some structure while changing something else.

In mathematics and computer science, different “mirrors” preserve different invariants. That’s why they behave differently.

For example:

Mirror type	Preserves	Changes
Physical mirror	Distances, shape	Orientation (reflection)
Translation	Structure	Location
Rotation	Distances	Angle
Scale	Shape ratios	Size
Analogy	Relationships	Domain
Compression	Meaning (approximately)	Representation
Translation (language)	Intent (ideally)	Words/syntax
Hash	Identity check	Recoverability (lost)

Each is a different mirror operator.

For the Pantheon, you could think of mirrors as a family of operators rather than one concept.

Mirror<T>
Input
   ↓
Transformation T
   ↓
Output
Invariant(T)

The important part isn’t the transformation.

It’s the invariant.

