Identity Mirror
    preserves object
Structural Mirror
    preserves topology
Behavioral Mirror
    preserves dynamics
Semantic Mirror
    preserves meaning
Functional Mirror
    preserves purpose
Historical Mirror
    preserves lineage
Probabilistic Mirror
    preserves distributions

Those become different operators.

⸻

An interesting consequence is that how a mirror is constructed determines what information is discarded.

For example:

Reflection
    loses handedness
Compression
    loses detail
Translation
    loses idioms
Summarization
    loses examples
Analogy
    loses literal correspondence

Every mirror is simultaneously:

* a preservation mechanism
* a deletion mechanism

The deleted information defines the mirror’s behavior.

⸻

That suggests a useful Pantheon abstraction.

Instead of asking:

“What mirror is this?”

Ask:

“What invariant does this mirror intentionally preserve?”

You could represent one as:

Mirror {
    operator
    preserved_invariants
    discarded_information
    reconstruction_rules
}

Then mirrors become composable.

Idea
  ↓
Semantic Mirror
  ↓
Code Mirror
  ↓
Mathematical Mirror
  ↓
Visualization Mirror

If each stage declares its invariants, you have a chain where the spirit of the idea is intentionally carried across representations, even though each representation necessarily discards some information. That turns “mirror” into a general computational mechanism for translating across domains while making explicit what is meant to remain unchanged.